   {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is a hair transplant procedure completely safe, and what are the clinical risks?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "Hair transplant is generally a safe procedure when it is performed by a qualified and experienced doctor in a properly equipped clinical setting. However, no medical procedure is completely risk-free. Possible risks include: Pain or discomfort Swelling Redness Itching Infection Bleeding Shock loss Poor graft growth Unnatural hairline Scarring Donor area thinning Delayed healing Reaction to medicines or anesthesia Most minor issues can be managed well when the procedure is done correctly and follow-up care is available. The bigger risks usually happen when surgery is done in unsafe settings, by unqualified people, without proper sterilization, or without medical supervision. This is why choosing the right clinic is so important."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What strict sterilization and hygiene protocols must a clinic maintain to prevent infections?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "A hair transplant clinic must maintain strict infection-control protocols because the procedure involves tiny openings in the scalp. A safe clinic should have: Clean and sterile procedure room Properly sterilized surgical instruments Disposable needles and consumables where required Sterile gloves, masks, caps, and gowns Proper patient draping Safe graft handling Clean graft storage solutions Proper biomedical waste disposal Pre-operative scalp cleaning Post-operative wound care instructions Trained staff following hygiene protocols The clinic should also screen for medical conditions that can increase infection risk, such as uncontrolled diabetes. Patients should never hesitate to ask about sterilization. A responsible clinic will answer clearly."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Who actually performs the procedure—the surgeon or the clinic technicians?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "This is one of the most important questions to ask before booking a hair transplant. In a safe and ethical clinic, the qualified surgeon should be directly involved in diagnosis, planning, hairline design, surgical decision-making, and supervision of the procedure. Technicians may assist with supportive tasks. But they should not independently perform major surgical steps without proper medical supervision. Before confirming your procedure, ask: Will the surgeon examine me personally? Will the surgeon design my hairline? Will the surgeon be present during surgery? Which steps will the surgeon perform? Which steps will the team assist with? If the clinic gives vague answers, be careful. At Neo Follicle, surgeon-led planning and patient safety are central to our process."
      }
     },
     {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do medical conditions like diabetes or hypertension impact the safety of my transplant?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
       "@type": "Answer",
       "text": "Medical conditions such as diabetes, hypertension, heart disease, thyroid disorders, bleeding tendencies, or autoimmune conditions can affect hair transplant safety and healing. This does not always mean you cannot undergo a hair transplant. But it means the condition must be properly evaluated and controlled before surgery. For example: Uncontrolled diabetes can increase infection risk and delay healing. High blood pressure can increase bleeding risk during the procedure. Blood thinners may need medical review before surgery. Heart conditions may require physician clearance. Scalp infections must be treated before transplant. Smoking can affect healing and graft survival. A good clinic will take your medical history seriously. It may advise blood tests, physician clearance, medication adjustments, or postponement if needed. Your safety is more important than rushing the procedure. At Neo Follicle, we always prefer to optimize the patient’s health before planning surgery. A safe transplant begins before the first graft is extracted."
      }
     }
